INT. HESTER'S KITCHEN, FARMHOUSE - EARLY EVENING

A low-beamed room, copper pots on hooks, a scrubbed pine table crowded with mismatched plates. Steam fogs the window. A cat sleeps on the radiator.

HESTER VANDERBILT, eighties, hunched in a flowered apron, stirs a pot with a wooden spoon. At the table sit her grandchildren: BAXTER, forty, in a rumpled suit; IMOGEN, thirty-five, with a toddler's drawing pinned to her cardigan; KENJI, thirty, fresh off a plane, backpack still on. Her great-grandchildren ROSALBA, eight, and TAVISH, five, kneel on their chairs, arranging peas into a pattern.

                    HESTER
          Everyone sit properly.

                    ROSALBA
          We are, Granny Hess.

                    TAVISH
          I'm sitting with my knees.

                    IMOGEN
          Tavish, honey, on your bottom.

He turns around, grinning, and sits on his bottom. Rosalba rolls her eyes and slides a pea toward him.

Baxter looks at his phone. Kenji glances at Baxter, then at the phone, then at his plate. Hester sets down the pot with a thump.

                    HESTER
          Baxter. The phone.

                    BAXTER
          Granny, it's work.

                    HESTER
          It's Sunday.

                    BAXTER
          It's always work.

                    KENJI
          Bax, give it to me.

He holds out his hand. Baxter hesitates, then drops the phone in it. Kenji slips it into his backpack and zips it closed. Nobody says anything. Imogen reaches for the potatoes.

                    IMOGEN
          I'll serve.

                    HESTER
          Imogen, sit. I'll serve.

                    IMOGEN
          Granny

                    HESTER
          Sit.

She sits. Hester ladles stew into bowls, one by one, her hands shaking slightly. Tavish watches, wide-eyed. Rosalba stands and takes the ladle gently from her great-grandmother's hands and finishes the serving herself, with the solemn care of a priest.

                    ROSALBA
          Granny Hess, you sit too.

Hester lowers herself into the chair at the head of the table. The family looks at her, then at one another, and no one reaches for a spoon.

                    TAVISH
          Are we saying thank you?

                    HESTER
          We are.

She folds her hands. Around the table, the others do the same, even Kenji, even Baxter, and the cat on the radiator opens one eye.
